  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nThe main weather concern with this forecast issuance will be \nprecipitation  very late tonight before lingering through \nThursday night (Thanksgiving). The latest guidance/ensemble data \nshows a couple pieces of upper-level energy pushing through the \ncentral CONUS; however, there is still considerable uncertainty on \nexactly how those two feature interact. That continues to result in \na very challenging forecast with many solutions possible depending \non how those features actually phase up (or not). Even if the \nphasing doesn't occur, guidance still deepens the northern stream \nupper low as it becomes centered over the Quad State region for \nWednesday night into Thursday. \n\nThe latest EFI continues to highlight locations across our far \nnorthern area and points north for an outlier type event, but that \ncould easily shift over the next day or two. The trend has been to \nshift the heavier band of snow farther east. That makes sense as the \nbest 850-700mb FGEN forcing has drifted eastward becoming more \nnorth/south oriented over the long axis of IL, even into western KY. \nInterestingly enough, mid level lapse rates also steepen up quite a \nbit as the upper low wraps up over the area, point toward \nsubstantial precipitation rates where the best banding sets up.\n\nReviewing the probabilistic data from multiple ensemble sources, \nshows that far northern portions of our area, from roughly Perry \nCounty MO northeast through Franklin to Edwards/Wabash Counties and \npoints north and northwest have the best chance of seeing an inch or \nmore of snow. In fact, there is roughly a 40% to 60% chance of \nseeing an inch or more of snow up that way and a 20% to 30% chance \nof 2 inches or more. The rest of the area could see some snowflakes \nmix in with the rain, but generally less than an inch of snow \nanticipated. Most of the accumulation would likely remain on grassy \nsurfaces/elevated surfaces due to the recent warm weather and warmer \nsoil temperatures (currently in the low to mid 40s). Definitely \nworth monitoring over the next few days!\n\nUnsettled weather may linger a bit longer as a zonal to troughed \npattern lingers nearby and shortwave energy passes through the flow. \nMoisture looks to be limited, but can't rule out some lingering \ncloud cover and possibly some light precipitation into Friday and \nSaturday before high pressure builds back into the region.",
